Notes

1.The Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm addresses the duty discussed in Maldonado. It reads:

389
§ 39. Duty Based on Prior Conduct Creating a Risk of Physical Harm

When an actor’s prior conduct, even though not tortious, creates a continuing risk of physical harm of a type characteristic of the conduct, the actor has a duty to exercise reasonable care to prevent or minimize the harm.

2.Does this duty rule add anything to the foundational risk-creation rule that if one’s conduct creates a risk, one owes a duty of reasonable care? Doesn’t reasonable care include warning, protecting, or rescuing others from the risk one created? The answer is that it does not. Recall that duties are act-centered, or perhaps more accurately, choice-centered. Courts are asking: “Assuming that the defendant chose his conduct unreasonably, should the court impose a duty to have chosen reasonably?” The choice at issue in a risk-creation scenario is the choice to do the conduct that created the risk. Application of the risk-creation rule to the Maldonado case would thus have led to a conclusion that because the railroad’s conduct—operating a railroad and shaking the car when Maldonado attempted to board it—created a risk of harm to Maldonado, the railroad owed a duty to have operated the railroad and shaken the car reasonably. The question of whether the railroad owed a subsequent duty to warn, protect, or rescue Maldonado from the risk that they had already created is an independent question governed by a separate, affirmative duty rule.

3.Continuing duty. Where the risk that the defendant created was of a continuing nature, the duty to protect against that risk continues. For example, in Tresemer v. Barke, 86 Cal.App.3d 656, 150 Cal.Rptr. 384 (1978), the defendant doctor implanted a Dalkon Shield intrauterine device at a time when the risks of such devices were unknown. Two years later, however, when the risks became known, the defendant failed to warn plaintiff of the risk. Although the defendant acted reasonably in initially creating the risk, the defendant owed a duty to warn of that risk so long as it existed.

What is the scope of this duty? If one person sells a used car to another and two years later finds out the car had a defective air-bag, did the seller have a duty to warn the buyer? Why or why not? What if a plumber installs a hot water heater in a home and several years later sees a notice that this brand of water heater is prone to catch fire?

